{
  "id": "lagrange-multipliers",
  "title": "Lagrange Multipliers: When Gradients Align",
  "eyebrow": "constrained extrema",
  "language": "en",
  "duration": 59,
  "topic": "Constrained extrema by Lagrange multipliers — at a maximum or minimum of f along a constraint g(x, y) = c, the gradient of f and the gradient of g are parallel. Slide a point along x² + y² = 2 with f = x + y; ∇f stays fixed pointing northeast, ∇g rotates with the point; the two vectors lock parallel at (1, 1) (max) and antiparallel at (−1, −1) (min). ∇f = λ∇g picks out the candidates.",
  "subject_id": "mat2b",
  "chapter_number": 6,
  "prerequisites": [
    "gradient-and-level-curves",
    "directional-derivative"
  ],
  "beats": [
    {
      "id": "manimoIntro",
      "start": 0,
      "end": 4.3,
      "narration": "Where does f get its biggest value if we are forced to stay on a curve?",
      "visuals": {
        "layout": "center",
        "elements": [
          {
            "type": "manimo-enter",
            "content": "Manimo bobs in centre-screen",
            "delay": 0
          },
          {
            "type": "text-caption",
            "content": "Pin a point on a curve — when is f largest?",
            "delay": 0.7,
            "color": "var(--chalk-100)",
            "position": "right of Manimo, serif italic"
          }
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "setup",
      "start": 4.3,
      "end": 16.63,
      "narration": "Take f equals x plus y, and constrain to the circle x squared plus y squared equals two. The level lines of f are diagonal — every step northeast pushes f up.",
      "visuals": {
        "layout": "left-right",
        "elements": [
          {
            "type": "graph-axes",
            "content": "xy axes",
            "delay": 0.2,
            "color": "var(--chalk-200)"
          },
          {
            "type": "graph-curve",
            "content": "Family of diagonal level lines x + y = c",
            "delay": 0.6,
            "color": "var(--chalk-300)"
          },
          {
            "type": "graph-curve",
            "content": "Constraint circle x² + y² = 2 (radius √2)",
            "delay": 1.6,
            "color": "var(--amber-400)"
          },
          {
            "type": "text-formula",
            "content": "f(x, y) = x + y",
            "delay": 2.2,
            "color": "var(--chalk-100)",
            "position": "right panel — display formula"
          },
          {
            "type": "text-formula",
            "content": "g(x, y) = x² + y² = 2",
            "delay": 3,
            "color": "var(--amber-300)",
            "position": "right panel — constraint"
          },
          {
            "type": "text-caption",
            "content": "Stay on the amber circle. Where is f biggest?",
            "delay": 4,
            "color": "var(--chalk-300)",
            "position": "right panel — sans caption"
          }
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "sliding",
      "start": 16.63,
      "end": 28.22,
      "narration": "Slide a point along the circle. The gradient of f is fixed — it points northeast everywhere. The gradient of g rotates with the point, always pointing straight out from the origin.",
      "visuals": {
        "layout": "left-right",
        "elements": [
          {
            "type": "graph-curve",
            "content": "Constraint circle held lit",
            "delay": 0,
            "color": "var(--amber-400)"
          },
          {
            "type": "graph-marker",
            "content": "Point P = (√2 cos θ, √2 sin θ) sliding around circle",
            "delay": 0.3,
            "color": "var(--chalk-100)"
          },
          {
            "type": "svg-path",
            "content": "∇f arrow at P, constant direction (1, 1)",
            "delay": 0.5,
            "color": "var(--violet-400)"
          },
          {
            "type": "svg-path",
            "content": "∇g arrow at P, radial (2x, 2y)",
            "delay": 0.7,
            "color": "var(--rose-400)"
          },
          {
            "type": "text-formula",
            "content": "∇f = (1, 1)",
            "delay": 1,
            "color": "var(--violet-400)",
            "position": "right panel"
          },
          {
            "type": "text-formula",
            "content": "∇g = (2x, 2y)",
            "delay": 1.6,
            "color": "var(--rose-400)",
            "position": "right panel"
          },
          {
            "type": "text-caption",
            "content": "Live readout: angle between ∇f and ∇g updates as P moves",
            "delay": 2,
            "color": "var(--chalk-300)",
            "position": "right panel — sans caption"
          }
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "alignment",
      "start": 28.22,
      "end": 41.02,
      "narration": "At the constrained maximum the two arrows line up. At the constrained minimum they point opposite ways. So at every constrained extremum, the gradient of f is a scalar multiple of the gradient of g.",
      "visuals": {
        "layout": "left-right",
        "elements": [
          {
            "type": "graph-marker",
            "content": "Point P snaps to (1, 1) — both arrows parallel",
            "delay": 0.3,
            "color": "var(--amber-300)"
          },
          {
            "type": "text-formula",
            "content": "P = (1, 1)  f = 2  ←  max",
            "delay": 1,
            "color": "var(--amber-300)",
            "position": "right panel — large"
          },
          {
            "type": "graph-marker",
            "content": "Point P slides to (-1, -1) — arrows antiparallel",
            "delay": 3,
            "color": "var(--rose-300)"
          },
          {
            "type": "text-formula",
            "content": "P = (−1, −1)  f = −2  ←  min",
            "delay": 3.8,
            "color": "var(--rose-300)",
            "position": "right panel — large"
          },
          {
            "type": "text-formula",
            "content": "∇f = λ ∇g",
            "delay": 5.5,
            "color": "var(--amber-300)",
            "position": "right panel — payoff line, serif italic, large"
          },
          {
            "type": "text-caption",
            "content": "λ is the Lagrange multiplier",
            "delay": 6.4,
            "color": "var(--chalk-300)",
            "position": "right panel — sans caption"
          }
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "solving",
      "start": 41.02,
      "end": 52.36,
      "narration": "Set ∇f equal to lambda times ∇g and solve. The system gives x equals y and lambda equals plus or minus one half, landing exactly on the two candidates.",
      "visuals": {
        "layout": "center",
        "elements": [
          {
            "type": "text-formula",
            "content": "(1, 1) = λ (2x, 2y)",
            "delay": 0.2,
            "color": "var(--chalk-200)",
            "position": "step one"
          },
          {
            "type": "text-formula",
            "content": "x = y,  λ = 1/(2x)",
            "delay": 1.2,
            "color": "var(--chalk-200)",
            "position": "step two"
          },
          {
            "type": "text-formula",
            "content": "x² + y² = 2  →  x = ±1",
            "delay": 2.2,
            "color": "var(--chalk-100)",
            "position": "constraint substituted"
          },
          {
            "type": "text-formula",
            "content": "(1, 1),  λ = ½     (−1, −1),  λ = −½",
            "delay": 3.4,
            "color": "var(--amber-300)",
            "position": "two candidates"
          }
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "takeaway",
      "start": 52.36,
      "end": 59,
      "narration": "At every constrained extremum, the gradients align — that is the whole trick.",
      "visuals": {
        "layout": "center",
        "elements": [
          {
            "type": "text-caption",
            "content": "Gradients align at the constrained extremum.",
            "delay": 0.1,
            "color": "var(--chalk-100)",
            "position": "centre, serif italic ~28px"
          }
        ]
      }
    }
  ]
